#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This guide demonstrates a minimal working example of the `qiskit-addon-sqd` "
"package. We use sample-based quantum diagonalization (SQD) to refine noisy "
"samples taken from a quantum circuit that approximates the ground state of a"
" chemistry Hamiltonian. Ground state energies are approximated by "
"diagonalizing the Hamiltonian in subspaces formed by the refined samples.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For more explanatory end-to-end workflows that run on quantum hardware, "
"check out the tutorials:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[Sample-based quantum diagonalization](/tutorials/sample-based-quantum-"
"diagonalization): chemistry Hamiltonian workflow\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[Sample-based Krylov quantum diagonalization](/tutorials/sample-based-"
"krylov-quantum-diagonalization): fermionic lattice model workflow\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Prepare the inputs for SQD {#1-prepare-the-inputs-for-sqd}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"First, we define the chemistry Hamiltonian and quantum circuit that "
"approximates its ground state. The system studied in this example is N₂ at "
"equilibrium geometry in STO-3G basis with two core orbitals frozen. This "
"system has ``8`` spatial orbitals and ``10`` electrons, which means the "
"quantum circuit is defined on ``16`` qubits, where qubits ``0-7`` represent "
"spin-up orbitals and qubits ``8-15`` represent spin-down orbitals. Since "
"this is a closed-shell system with ``10`` electrons, we know each "
"physically-valid sample should have hamming weight ``10``, where the hamming"
" weight of both the left and right halves of the bitstring is ``5``.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "2. Generate noisy samples {#2-generate-noisy-samples}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In a real workflow you would design an ansatz circuit, optimize it for "
"hardware, and sample it on the QPU. Here we skip that and draw ``10,000`` "
"uniformly random bitstrings instead. While our samples contain no "
"information about the actual ground state, we will see that the SQD protocol"
" can still recover the true ground state by iteratively refining these "
"samples, diagonalizing, and using the energies at each iteration to inform "
"the next round of bitstring refinement. We can eventually recover the exact "
"ground state in this manner because the subspace containing the ground state"
" is small enough to calculate exactly. Specifically, since we know each half"
" of each bitstring (``8`` bits) should have hamming weight ``5``, we know "
"the full Hilbert subspace of the system is size $\\binom{8}{5}^2=3136$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "3. Post-process with SQD {#3-post-process-with-sqd}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The objective of the SQD algorithm is to refine noisy samples taken from a "
"QPU and ultimately diagonalize the Hamiltonian in a subspace spanned by "
"those refined samples. This is done iteratively as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Refine samples by postselecting only those which have correct particle "
"number for both the spin-up and spin-down subsystems\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Diagonalize in small subspaces spanned by the refined samples and collect "
"information about the subspaces that yield the the best (lowest) energies.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For each iteration:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '    - '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Refine the noisy samples by probabilistically flipping bits in samples with "
"incorrect particle number. The probabilities are calculated using the "
"subspace information from the most recent diagonalization.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '    - '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Diagonalize in small subspaces spanned by the refined samples\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '    - '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Collect information about the subspaces yielding the best (lowest) "
"energies\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We can see from the output that the algorithm converges to the exact ground "
"state energy after ``6`` iterations and that the diagonalization is "
"performed over the full Hilbert subspace of dimension "
"$\\binom{8}{5}^2=3136$. Of course, diagonalizing over the full subspace is "
"usually not feasible, and therefore, systems with ground states having "
"sparse support are the most likely to benefit from subspace diagonalization "
"techniques such as SQD.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For more information on the diagonalize_fermionic_hamiltonian function, "
"visit the [API docs](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-addon-"
"sqd/fermion#diagonalize_fermionic_hamiltonian).\n"
msgstr ""
